<speak>
  <p>Welcome to Lesson 2 of your Arizona Administrative Law Study Guide. Today we explore how agencies make rules, where that power comes from, and how the public participates.</p>
  <p>Agencies have no inherent power to make law. They can create binding rules only if the Legislature grants that authority through an <emphasis level="moderate">enabling statute</emphasis>.</p>
  <p><emphasis level="moderate">Quick Quiz 1.</emphasis> An agency’s rulemaking authority comes from which source? A) Its own discretion.  B) Its enabling statute.  C) Executive order.</p>
  <break time="2s"/>
  <p>Answer: B – the enabling statute gives legal permission to adopt rules. Without it, any rule is <emphasis level="moderate">ultra vires</emphasis>, beyond its power.</p>
  <p>Rules appear in several types under A.R.S. Section 41-1001: substantive rules, procedural rules, and interpretive rules or policy statements. Substantive rules have the force of law; policy statements merely explain how an agency interprets a statute.</p>
  <p><emphasis level="moderate">Quick Quiz 2.</emphasis> Which rule carries the force of law? A) Substantive rule.  B) Policy statement.  C) Internal memo.</p>
  <break time="2s"/>
  <p>Answer: A – only substantive rules adopted through proper APA procedures are legally binding.</p>
</speak>
